From 029ebcaa451ec6bb4517a708ae80ba5945a15249 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 08:28:32 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [SDL3-DOS] dos: patch 0145 -- lock the whole code image for
 ISRs; DOS_LockCode no longer trusts function layout

WHY DOS NEEDS THIS: every DOS_LockCode(fn, fn_End) in this series
assumes the compiler lays functions out in source order, with an empty
fn_End() right after fn(). DJGPP gcc 12 at -O2 does not: it emits
functions in its own call-graph order. Measured in this series' own
objects:
  - Hub 0143 build, SDL_dosaudio_sb.c.obj (one .text section):
      RingCopyOut 0xd80, RingCopyOut_End 0x0 (length -3456)
      SoundBlasterIRQHandler 0x6140, its _End 0x6110 (length -48)
      ISR_GetDMAHalf 0xe00, _End 0x6120 (+21280, spanning unrelated code)
      SfxSynthISRMix 0x10, _End 0x6130 (+24864, same)
  - With 0144 on top, 0144's MidiDeferWriteOne -> MidiDeferDrain_End
    range is positive, but MidiDeferDrain itself is inlined into
    SoundBlasterIRQHandler, outside that range.
  - SDL_dos_audio_synth.c (OPL-PIT and PC-speaker ISRs) and
    SDL_dosevents.c (keyboard ISR) happen to come out in source order, so
    their ranges are positive.
So the SB IRQ handler -- the ISR every SB port runs -- was never locked.
Nothing noticed, for two reasons:
  - CWSDPMI accepts the wrapped length: a scratch probe calling
    _go32_dpmi_lock_code(fn, (unsigned long)-48) under CWSDPMI r7 in
    DOSBox-X gets rc=0. What, if anything, that call locks is not visible
    from the return code.
  - Even a correct per-function range cannot cover what the handlers call
    outside their own object. The 0143 SB handler (RingCopyOut inlined)
    calls libc memcpy, libgcc __udivdi3, SfxSynthISRMix, and the port's
    SDL/0072 MIDI tick callback through a function pointer. None of these
    was in any locked range, whatever the layout.

Remedies considered:
  - A dedicated ISR text section with locked bounds. DJGPP's default
    linker script (ld --verbose) folds *(.text.*) into .text with no SORT
    and no __start_/__stop_ symbols, so the bounds would need a custom
    linker script in every port. It also still would not cover libc,
    libgcc or port callbacks.
  - -fno-toplevel-reorder / -fno-reorder-functions on the ISR files. This
    keeps depending on compiler layout (now through flags every port's
    SDL build would have to carry), and still would not cover callees in
    other objects.
  - Min/max over the known symbols. That gives no end for a function body
    at all, and has the same callee gap.
  - Chosen: lock the program's whole code image once, [start, etext),
    before the first handler is installed.
      - `start` is crt0's entry symbol, the linker script's ENTRY.
      - `etext` is PROVIDEd by the linker script at the end of .text,
        which also holds .text.unlikely, .rodata and the rest of the
        linker's .text input.
    Both come from the link, not from the compiler's function order. It
    covers every ISR, everything it calls (libc, libgcc, SDL, port
    callbacks) and any cold split, in any layout.
Cost: the code image can no longer be swapped out; CWSDPMI loads the
image whole at startup anyway. It is about 1.5 MB for a small SDL
program; the line below logs the exact size.

WHAT:
  - DOS_LockProgramText() (SDL_dos.c) locks [start, etext) once and logs
    "DOS: patch 0145 program code lock [start=..., etext=...) = N bytes:
    locked|FAILED". DOS_HookInterrupt() and DOS_HookInterruptReplace() --
    the only two ways any ISR in this series is installed -- call it
    first.
  - DOS_LockCode() keeps its name and call sites, and now goes through
    DOS_LockCodeRange(). A positive range is locked exactly as before. A
    range with length <= 0 is not passed to the DPMI host; it is logged
    once per call site as the permanent witness:
      "DOS: patch 0145 DOS_LockCode(<fn>) range length <n> <= 0 (end
      marker laid out before the function); not locked by range, covered
      by the program code lock (held|NOT held)"
  - Data locks (DOS_LockData / DOS_LockVariable) are unchanged.

Scope, honestly: never observed failing. The failure mode being closed
is a page fault inside an ISR, which needs CWSDPMI to actually page. It
does that only when physical memory runs out and a swap file is
available; its own documentation lists "an illegal page fault happens in
a RMCB or HW interrupt (lock all pages!)" as a cause of its "Page fault"
abort.

Validation (DJGPP gcc 12.2.0, this series 0001-0145, DOSBox-X only):
  - Built objects: the per-function ranges come out as before
    (SoundBlasterIRQHandler -64, RingCopyOut -3504; the rest positive;
    this patch does not change layout). In a linked probe, [start=0x19f0,
    etext=0x1707e8) contains every ISR entry point in the series
    (SoundBlasterIRQHandler, SfxSynthISRMix, RingCopyOut, ISR_GetDMAHalf,
    MidiDeferWriteOne, OplPitISR, OplPitBeepISR, OplPitChainOld,
    BeepStartNote, KeyboardIRQHandler) and each callee checked: memcpy,
    __udivdi3, __divdi3, the port's tick callback, SDL_DOSOpl3WriteRegister,
    SDL_DOSMpu401WriteByte, SDL_DOSMidiDeferCapture. No ISR has a .cold
    split in that build.
  - Runtime, dosbox-run.sh --sb-live, all three hook paths:
      keyboard at video init: the code lock is logged first, "locked",
        about 1.5 MB;
      SB: the witness logs exactly the two negative ranges, then audio
        plays as on 0143 -- the SBRING PCM probe gives 43/43 and 32/32
        chunk accounting and 0 overwrites under a 392400-call pump storm;
        the 0144 MIDI probe gives 172/172 deferred writes on time;
      OPL-PIT: the pump starts, 100 ISR ticks in 1 s, BIOS chains 18/18.
  - Hardware not run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Nz9XeKeVG8Ab7dm8esJsMu
---
 src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c | 75 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h | 17 ++++++++--
 2 files changed, 90 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
index 5bfabcfeb..0e958fa8d 100644
--- a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
+++ b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.c
@@ -87,11 +87,85 @@ char *DOS_GetFarPtrCString(const Uint32 segoffset)
     return retval;
 }
 
+/* DOS-PORT patch 0145: keep ISR code resident without trusting function
+   layout.
+
+   Every DOS_LockCode(fn, fn_End) in this series assumed the compiler lays
+   functions out in source order. DJGPP gcc 12 at -O2 does not: in
+   SDL_dosaudio_sb.c the end markers of the SB IRQ handler and RingCopyOut
+   land BEFORE their functions, so those ranges had negative lengths (the
+   file's other two pairs span unrelated code instead). CWSDPMI
+   accepts the wrapped length with rc=0, so nothing ever reported it. And
+   even a correct range cannot cover what the handlers call outside their
+   own object -- libc memcpy, libgcc __udivdi3, the port's SDL/0072 MIDI
+   tick callback through a function pointer.
+
+   So: lock the program's whole code image once, [start, etext) -- crt0's
+   entry symbol (the linker script's ENTRY) to the linker-provided end of
+   .text, which also covers .rodata -- before the first handler is
+   installed. Those bounds come from the link, not from the compiler's
+   function order. The cost is that the code image can never be swapped
+   out; CWSDPMI only pages when physical memory runs out, and the image is
+   loaded whole at startup anyway. Data locks (DOS_LockData /
+   DOS_LockVariable) are unaffected: variables are not reordered relative
+   to their own sizes.
+
+   Failure mode being closed: a page fault inside an ISR, which only
+   happens if CWSDPMI actually pages (its own docs: "an illegal page fault
+   happens in a RMCB or HW interrupt (lock all pages!)"). Never observed
+   failing in this series. */
+extern void dos_port_dbg_log(const char *fmt, ...);
+extern char dos_port_text_start[] __asm__("start");
+extern char etext[];
+
+bool DOS_LockProgramText(void)
+{
+    static bool done = false;
+    static bool ok = false;
+    if (done) {
+        return ok;
+    }
+    done = true;
+    const unsigned long len = (unsigned long)(etext - dos_port_text_start);
+    const int rc = ((unsigned long)etext > (unsigned long)dos_port_text_start) ? _go32_dpmi_lock_code(dos_port_text_start, len) : -1;
+    ok = (rc == 0);
+    dos_port_dbg_log("DOS: patch 0145 program code lock [start=0x%lx, etext=0x%lx) = %lu bytes: %s",
+                     (unsigned long)dos_port_text_start, (unsigned long)etext, len,
+                     ok ? "locked" : "FAILED (ISR code is pageable)");
+    return ok;
+}
+
+/* The runtime witness for the layout problem: any DOS_LockCode range whose
+   end marker sits at or before its start is logged once per call site and
+   not passed to the DPMI host. Positive ranges behave exactly as before. */
+int DOS_LockCodeRange(void *start, void *end, const char *what)
+{
+    static const char *reported[32];
+    static int nreported = 0;
+    const long len = (long)((char *)end - (char *)start);
+    if (len > 0) {
+        return _go32_dpmi_lock_code(start, (unsigned long)len);
+    }
+    for (int i = 0; i < nreported; i++) {
+        if (reported[i] == what) {
+            return -1;
+        }
+    }
+    if (nreported < (int)SDL_arraysize(reported)) {
+        reported[nreported++] = what;
+    }
+    dos_port_dbg_log("DOS: patch 0145 DOS_LockCode(%s) range length %ld <= 0 (end marker laid out before "
+                     "the function); not locked by range, covered by the program code lock (%s)",
+                     what, len, DOS_LockProgramText() ? "held" : "NOT held");
+    return -1;
+}
+
 void DOS_HookInterrupt(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook)
 {
     SDL_assert(irq >= 0 && irq <= 15);
     SDL_assert(fn != NULL);
     SDL_assert(hook != NULL);
+    (void)DOS_LockProgramText(); // DOS-PORT patch 0145: before any handler can run
     hook->fn = fn;
     hook->irq = irq;
     hook->interrupt_vector = DOS_IRQToVector(irq);
@@ -151,6 +225,7 @@ void DOS_HookInterruptReplace(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook
     SDL_assert(irq >= 0 && irq <= 15);
     SDL_assert(fn != NULL);
     SDL_assert(hook != NULL);
+    (void)DOS_LockProgramText(); // DOS-PORT patch 0145: before any handler can run
     hook->fn = fn;
     hook->irq = irq;
     hook->interrupt_vector = DOS_IRQToVector(irq);
diff --git a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
index 457cffe2d..39b8962e4 100644
--- a/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
+++ b/src/core/dos/SDL_dos.h
@@ -49,9 +49,18 @@
 
 // Lock a range of code so it won't be paged out during interrupts.
 // Usage: DOS_LockCode(function_name, function_end_label)
-// The function_end_label must be defined immediately after the function.
+// The function_end_label is defined immediately after the function in the
+// SOURCE, but gcc -O2 does not keep source order: it emits functions in its
+// own call-graph order, and the end marker can land before the function
+// (the SB IRQ handler and RingCopyOut in SDL_dosaudio_sb.c under DJGPP
+// gcc 12; the file's other pairs span unrelated code instead). A
+// range like that is never passed to the DPMI host (a wrapped length is
+// accepted with rc=0 by CWSDPMI, so the failure was silent); it is logged
+// once instead. What keeps ISR code resident is DOS_LockProgramText(),
+// which DOS_HookInterrupt*() run before any handler is installed. See
+// patch 0145.
 #define DOS_LockCode(start, end) \
-    _go32_dpmi_lock_code((void *)(start), (char *)(end) - (char *)(start))
+    DOS_LockCodeRange((void *)(start), (void *)(end), #start)
 
 // Lock a range of data so it won't be paged out during interrupts.
 #define DOS_LockData(var, size) \
@@ -154,6 +163,10 @@ typedef struct DOS_InterruptHook
     bool replaced;
 } DOS_InterruptHook;
 
+/* DOS-PORT patch 0145: see DOS_LockCode above. */
+int DOS_LockCodeRange(void *start, void *end, const char *what);
+bool DOS_LockProgramText(void);
+
 void DOS_HookInterrupt(int irq, DOS_InterruptHookFn fn, DOS_InterruptHook *hook); // `irq` is the IRQ number, not the interrupt vector number!
 
 /* DOSKUTSU patch (Phase 11): variant of DOS_HookInterrupt that REPLACES
-- 
